# Yoodle — environment template.
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in real values. `.env` is gitignored and MUST stay that way.
# Never hardcode a secret in source. See SECURITY.md.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_* values are embedded in the client bundle and are therefore
# PUBLIC. Anything that must stay secret (Stripe secret key, Supabase service
# role key) belongs on a server or in EAS secrets — never in this file's
# EXPO_PUBLIC_ section and never in the app bundle.

# --- Supabase (phase 2) ---
# Project Settings -> API. Both values are PUBLIC by design: they are compiled
# into the app bundle and readable by anyone who downloads it. The publishable
# (anon) key is safe ONLY because Row Level Security is enabled on every table.
#
# NOTE: current Supabase docs name this PUBLISHABLE_KEY, and new projects issue
# keys prefixed `sb_publishable_`. Older projects call the same thing the "anon"
# key. Either value goes here.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
#
# NEVER add the service_role / secret key here. It bypasses Row Level Security
# entirely. It belongs only in Edge Functions and the dashboard.

# --- Maps (phase 4) ---
# NOT NEEDED YET, and deliberately not wired up. react-native-maps works in Expo
# Go with no key at all, and on iOS it uses Apple Maps, which never needs one.
#
# A key becomes necessary only for an ANDROID dev build or store build, where
# maps render blank tiles without it. Wiring it up also means moving app.json to
# app.config.ts, because static JSON cannot read process.env. Left for whichever
# phase first produces an Android build.
#
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_ANDROID_KEY=

# --- Added in a later phase (Stripe Connect) ---
# EXPO_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
